<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_eq-challenge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Challenge - Representing Potential</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->
	
	<project>
		<title>Infinite Wires</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				Two infinite wires are located parallel to the y-axis. One wire has uniform charge density <m>+\lambda</m> and is located at <m>x = +x_o</m>. The other wire has uniform charge density <m>-\lambda</m> and is located at <m>x = -x_o</m>. The electric potential in the <m>xy</m>-plane for this system is given by: <me>
					V(x, y) = 2k \lambda \ln{|x + x_o |} - 2k \lambda \ln{|x-x_o |} = 2k \lambda \ln{| \frac{x + x_o }{x - x_o} |}
				</me>
				Calculate the electric field at every point in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
			</p>

			<assemblage>
				<p><alert><!--REPLACE_TAG-->Tip:</alert> For your physical representation (part 1c), create an accurate equipotential graph in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the corresponding electric field vector map.</p>
			</assemblage>
			<assemblage>
				<p><alert><!--REPLACE_TAG-->Tip:</alert> For your symbolic sensemaking (part 3c), show that your electric field is what you expect from the superposition of two infinite line charges.</p>
			</assemblage>
		</statement>
	</project>

	<investigation>
		<title>Equipotential Graph II</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_equipot-2">
				<caption>Equipotential graph in the <m>xy</m>-plane.</caption>
				<image source="elec/equipotential-graph-2.png" width = "100%" >
					<description>An equipotential map with many concentric circles at the top and many ellipses at the bottom.  A, B, and C lie on the same nearly circular contour near the top, while C, D, and E lie in a straight line from top to bottom. </description>
				</image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				This problem is a modified Explanation Task with several specific prompts. Above is a graph of the equipotentials due to some unknown distribution of charges, with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> measured in meters. The boxed numbers represent the values of the potential in volts.
			</p>
		
			<ol marker="1.">
				<li>
					<p>
						Sketch vectors at the indicated points that represent the electric field <m>\vec{E}</m>. Both the direction and the magnitude of your vectors should be qualitatively correct. Describe how you determined your answers.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Explicitly rank the magnitudes of the electric field vectors you drew. Describe how you determined your answers.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Sketch a charge diagram, using <m>+</m> and <m>-</m> to indicate the locations where you expect there to be positive and negative charges, respectively. Justify your answers.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Imagine that you are a charged particle moving along the vertical line that passes through points C, D, and E (the <m>y</m>-axis).
					</p>
					<ol marker="a.">
						<li>
							<p>
								Sketch a graph of the electric potential <m>V</m> as a function of <m>y</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								On a different set of axes, sketch the <m>y</m>-component of the electric field <m>E_y</m> as a function of <m>y</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Discuss the relationship between your two graphs.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
	</investigation>



	<subsubsection>
		<title>Metacognitive Reflection</title>
		<activity>
			<title>Options for Calculating the Electric Field</title>
			
			<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				You now have a few different ways to calculate the electric field from the electric potential. Describe how you can determine the electric field if you are given: 1) an equation for the potential everywhere in space, or 2) an equipotential map. Be sure to discuss how to determine the direction in both cases. 
			</p>
		</statement>
		</activity>
		
	</subsubsection>


<!--NO AI-->
</section>

